  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — every day marks a habit, so the present simple is required, and he takes goes. Go would need a plural subject; is going and has gone would describe a single occasion or a finished action.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — Look! fixes the action at this moment, and an action in progress takes the present continuous: is crying. Cries is a habit, cried is past, and has cried is finished.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — yesterday is a finished time, which takes the simple past saw. Have seen cannot sit with a finished time; had seen would need a second past event; see is present.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the ringing interrupted an action already in progress, so the past continuous were sleeping. Slept would make the two events simultaneous; have slept is present perfect; had slept would put the sleep before the ringing.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — since plus a point in time pulls the present perfect, has lived. Lived would close the period; lives suggests a permanent fact with no sense of duration; is living suggests a temporary arrangement.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — two past events, and the starting came first, so the past perfect had started. Has started is present perfect; started would make the events simultaneous; was starting would mean it was in the act of starting.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — the finishing happened before his arrival, so the past perfect is exact. Finished would leave the order unclear; have finished is present perfect; was finishing would overlap the arrival.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the first conditional uses present in the if-clause and will in the main clause: will stay. Would stay belongs to the second conditional; stayed and would have stayed belong to the third.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the second conditional takes would in the main clause. Will would mix a present modal with an unreal past; would have belongs to the third conditional; am going to states a plan rather than a hypothetical.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the third conditional takes had in the if-clause and would have in the main clause: would have caught. Would catch mixes the past clause with a present result; caught and will catch break the pattern completely.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — a past reporting verb moves the reported verb back one step, so is becomes was. Has been and will be are present forms, and is is the original tense, which reported speech does not keep.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — the day before places the posting before the report, so the past perfect had posted is required. Posted would leave the order unclear; has posted is present perfect; was posting would describe an action in progress.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the results cannot announce anything, so the passive is needed, and yesterday makes it past: were announced. Announced and are announcing would make the results the announcer; have announced is present perfect.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the letter receives the typing, so the passive is needed, and it happened before the meeting, so the past perfect passive: had been typed. The other three all make the letter the typist.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the bridge is the thing built, so the future passive will be built is required. Will build and builds make the bridge the builder, and is building is present continuous active.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — after has the past participle gone is required. Went is the past tense, go is the base form, and going is the present participle.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — after has the participle done is required. Did is past tense, and do and does are present forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — so far reaches up to the present, which pulls the present perfect has written. Wrote would need a finished time; had written would need a second past event; writes is a habit.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — last week is a finished time, so the simple past met is correct. Have met cannot sit with it, had met would need a second past event, and meet is present.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — the departure came before our arrival in the past, so had left. Left would make the two simultaneous; has left is present perfect; was leaving would mean it was still pulling out.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the light failure interrupted an action in progress, so the past continuous was cooking. Cooked would make the events simultaneous; has cooked is present perfect; had cooked would put the cooking first.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — the waiting ran up to a past moment, which is the past perfect continuous had been waiting. Waited gives no duration; were waiting would suggest the wait was interrupted rather than measured; have waited is present perfect.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    C — for plus a period reaching the present pulls the present perfect: has worked. Works and is working describe the present only; worked would close the period.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — usually marks a habit, so the present simple is required, and she takes gets. Get needs a plural subject; is getting suggests a temporary arrangement; has got means has obtained.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — a singular subject takes a singular present verb, and tomorrow with a timetable takes the present simple meets. Meet needs a plural subject, is meeting with adds a preposition the sentence does not have, and have met is plural and perfect.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — wish about the present takes the past subjunctive had. Have is the real present; will have and would have are future or conditional forms that wish does not take.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — if only about the past takes the past perfect had studied. Studied would make the regret present; studies is present; would study does not follow if only.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — it is time takes the past subjunctive left, because the leaving is already overdue. Leave and will leave treat it as a future plan; have left is present perfect and does not follow this frame.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — hardly is followed by when. Than belongs to no sooner; then is an adverb of time and cannot join the clauses; that does not follow hardly in this construction.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — no sooner takes had and inverts with the subject: no sooner had he sat down. Did and has do not form the past perfect, and was cannot take sat.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — deny takes a gerund, so taking. To take would follow refuse or agree; take and took are finite forms and cannot follow denied in this sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — enjoy takes a gerund: reading. To read follows want or hope, not enjoy; read and reads are finite forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the checking found the sleeping in progress, so the past continuous was sleeping. Slept states a completed fact; has slept is present perfect; had slept would put the sleep before the checking.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — by next June marks completion in the future, so the future perfect will have worked. Will work states a future fact; have worked is present perfect; would work is conditional.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the road is being worked on now, so the present continuous passive is being repaired is required. Repairs and is repairing make the road the repairer, and has repaired is perfect and active.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — must followed by a perfect infinitive expresses a deduction about the past: must have heard. Hear refers to the present; be hearing is continuous and odd here; heard cannot follow must in this sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — ought to plus a perfect infinitive expresses a duty that was not met: ought to have arrived. To arrive refers to the present or future, and arrive and arriving cannot follow ought without to.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — the middle voice uses the active form for a general truth about how something sells: these books sell well. Are sold would need an agent and a particular transaction; are selling describes a temporary situation; have sold would make the books the seller.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — since 2010 reaches to the present, so the present perfect have been is required. Are would lose the duration; were would close the period; had been would need a past reference point.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 14.77pt)[
    B — would rather followed by a different subject takes the past subjunctive: I would rather you left. Leave is the bare form used when the subject is the same; will leave and have left do not follow the frame.
  ]

]

